Each coin was drawn with probability $\frac{1}{2}$. The fair coin shows heads with probability $\frac{1}{2}$, so its weight is $\frac{1}{4}$. The two-headed coin always shows heads, so its weight is $\frac{1}{2}$.

The total weight on heads is $\frac{1}{4} + \frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{4}$, and the fair coin's share of it is $\frac{1}{4}$.

P(\text{fair} \mid \text{heads}) = \frac{1/4}{3/4} = \frac{1}{3}
